[GTK][WPE] Composite layers whose CSS filter moves pixels instead of blurring on the main thread

WebChromeClient::allowedCompositingTriggers() leaves FilterTrigger out for every
WebKit2 port, so RenderLayerCompositor::requiresCompositingForFilters() never
promotes a layer for its `filter:` alone and the filter is applied in software,
inside the layer paint, on the web process main thread. On Cocoa that path runs
on Core Image and is cheap. On the Skia ports it is SkBlurImageFilter on the CPU.

Measured on a msm8998 phone (webkitgtk 2.52.6, GTK4/Wayland, Skia CPU rendering)
playing m.youtube.com with ambient mode -- a 720x675 div with `filter: blur(40px)`
and a 1.5x2.5 2D transform, showing a blurred copy of the video behind the
player: every 7-12 s the web process main thread spends 1.4-3.2 s in

    SkBlurEngine / SkBlurImageFilter::onFilterImage
    FEGaussianBlurSkiaApplier::apply
    FilterEffect::apply
    RenderLayer::updateFilterPaintingStrategy / paintLayerByApplyingTransform
    RenderLayer::paintLayerWithEffects / paintList

(eu-stack on the running process, symbolized). For that long the whole window
stops: no wl_surface commits from the UI process, the media element stops
counting frames. Ten such holes in a 100 s window, with the compositor and every
other thread idle in between.

Coordinated Graphics already composites filters: GraphicsLayerCoordinated::setFilters()
takes anything but reference filters and TextureMapper::applyFilters() runs them
as GPU passes on the compositor thread, downsampling for large blur radii. Only
the trigger is missing. Forcing the same page's filtered elements into layers
from the inspector (will-change: transform) makes the holes disappear: the
longest commit gap in 100 s goes from 3.2 s to 0.29 s.

Doing that for every filter is not free, though: the same page has 47 filtered
elements, mostly `brightness(0.9)` on thumbnails, and each composited filter
costs an intermediate surface per frame -- the compositor's frame period rose
from 34 to 45 ms with all 47 promoted. A colour-matrix filter is one cheap pass
per pixel in software; only the filters that move pixels (blur, drop-shadow) are
the ones whose software cost scales into seconds. So enable FilterTrigger on
USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS) and, on that path, require a pixel-moving filter.
Reference filters stay in software: GraphicsLayerCoordinated cannot composite
them anyway.

--- a/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebCoreSupport/WebChromeClient.h
+++ b/Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebCoreSupport/WebChromeClient.h
@@ -295,6 +295,11 @@
             CanvasTrigger |
 #if PLATFORM(COCOA) || USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
             ScrollableNonMainFrameTrigger |
+#endif
+#if USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
+            // TextureMapper applies filters as GPU passes on the compositor thread; the
+            // software path (FilterEffect on Skia CPU) runs inside the main-thread layer paint.
+            FilterTrigger |
 #endif
 #if PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY)
             AnimatedOpacityTrigger | // Allow opacity animations to trigger compositing mode for iPhone: <rdar://problem/7830677>
--- a/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/rendering/RenderLayerCompositor.cpp
@@ -3973,7 +3973,17 @@ bool RenderLayerCompositor::requiresCompositingForFilters(RenderLayerModelObject
     if (!(m_compositingTriggers & ChromeClient::FilterTrigger))
         return false;
 
+#if USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
+    // A composited filter costs an intermediate surface every frame, which a colour-matrix
+    // filter on a thumbnail does not earn. The filters worth a layer are the ones whose
+    // software cost is a blur over the layer's area, i.e. the ones that move pixels; reference
+    // filters cannot be composited by GraphicsLayerCoordinated at all.
+    if (!renderer.hasFilter())
+        return false;
+    return renderer.style().filter().hasFilterThatMovesPixels() && !renderer.style().filter().hasReferenceFilter();
+#else
     return renderer.hasFilter();
+#endif
 }
 
 bool RenderLayerCompositor::requiresCompositingForWillChange(RenderLayerModelObject& renderer) const
